      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fasten off, leaving a 15 cm tail. Thread through a tapestry needle and weave through 4 stitches on the back of round 8, reverse direction through 3 stitches. Trim. Weave in the magic ring tail along round 1. Block on a foam mat: pin the four corners, then ease each side and add a pin at every second cluster group. Steam generously and leave flat until completely dry."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Joining" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The eight-round square joins at its four corner ch-2 spaces and its 28 side ch-1 spaces. For a blanket with a plain treble border, work the border in joined rounds directly around the outside of a grid of eight-round squares. A single square at 20 cm fits a small cushion form (25 x 25 cm) with a plain treble border on two rounds."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Care" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Handwash in cool water with mild soap, or machine-wash on a delicates cycle. Do not tumble-dry. Lay flat and re-ease to shape while damp; the large size means it may need pinning to stay flat while it dries."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "What to try next" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Once the eight-round square is solid, try the two-colour or three-colour version — the same structure but with yarn changes between rounds creates a striped radial effect that reads differently from every granny square you have made before. The hexagon motifs use the same cluster-based construction adapted for six corners instead of four."
          }
        ]
      }
